\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Heller's two-time-slice transfer sketch}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Heller explicitly calls Appendix B a sketch. It treats
staggered fermions with the standard flavor-degenerate
mass and Wilson gauge action, pairs times #{2j} and
#{2j+1}, and uses temporal gauge with unit time links.
Its CAR and coherent-state formulas are part of that
construction \citet{Appendix B, (B.1)-(B.9), pp. 21-22}{heller1997schrodinger}.}

\p{Writing the source's transfer operators as
#{\transferop}, #{\transferop_G} and #{\transferop_F} to
distinguish them from lattice translations, the displayed
factorization is
##{\transferop=\transferop_G^{1/2}\transferop_F^\dagger
       \transferop_G\transferop_F\transferop_G^{1/2}.}
The dagger is the Hilbert-space adjoint in the source.
The text identifies this operator as self-adjoint and
positive, with #{\transferop=\ue^{-2a_tH}}
\citet{(B.10)-(B.12), pp. 22-23}{heller1997schrodinger}.
The factor #{2} describes two fine temporal spacings,
not a one-fine-step positivity assertion.}

\p{The Schrödinger-functional setup has an even number
of time slices, an odd labeled time extent with sites
from zero through that extent, and an even spatial extent
with twisted periodic spatial boundaries
\citet{Section 2, (2.6), p. 3}{heller1997schrodinger}.
Parity of the [torus time period](fdip-007M) alone does not
identify these boundaries. On a periodic circle,
nontrivial holonomy also prevents setting every temporal
link to the identity. The pure-gauge transfer factor,
slice pairing and observable correspondence remain
additional data for a cochain construction.}
